    {
      "number": 14677,
      "title": "[css-fonts-4] Define font feature value serialization",
      "url": "https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/14677",
      "author": "domenic",
      "mergedAt": "2026-11-02T13:28:20Z",
      "hint": "feature",
      "release": "⏳ 未リリース",
      "body": "Populate feature-value maps in source order, retaining each name's first position and last valid value. Specify serialization as an algorithm with fixed block order and exact whitespace and punctuation, including empty rules and the `font-display` descriptor. Place `@historical-forms` after the six block types implemented by browsers, and update the duplicate-name example to show the exact output.\n\nFixes #14658.\n\n---\n\nAs requested by @svgeesus. Also CC @emilio.\n\nGPT-6 Astra Extra High summary of this algorithm vs. current browsers, mainly based on corresponding WPT results:\n\nBehavior | Chromium 156 | Firefox 157 | WebKit 27.2\n-- | -- | -- | --\nProposed block order | Different order | Matches | Matches\nName order and duplicate replacement within one block | Matches | Matches | Matches\nName order across repeated blocks | Differs | Matches | Matches\nIgnore invalid declarations individually | Fails our case | Matches | Matches\nExact spaces and punctuation | Matches | Uses newlines instead | Matches\nfont-display and @historical-forms | Omitted | Omitted | Omitted\n\nSo the proposal matches Firefox and WebKit’s tested ordering behavior, and Chromium and WebKit’s whitespace formatting. It additionally specifies serialization for two already-specced features that these browser builds omit.",
      "files": [
        "css-fonts-4/Overview.bs (+105 -12)"
      ],
      "filesTotal": 1
    },
    {
